    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. My main concern is the risk of a stack shifting or tipping unexpectedly during the loading process. Sometimes even with a low center of gravity, things just seem to get knocked out of alignment or become unstable for no obvious reason. What are the most common causes of that kind of sudden instability in high-density setups?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sudden instability in high-density racking often stems from uneven beam spacing under the beams, causing uneven load distribution that leads to structural vibrations or tipping. Furthermore, any vibration from forklifts, ramps, or nearby equipment can have a disproportionately large effect when pallets are tight against one another with no clearance. Lastly, if beams are not truly flat at the intersection points, the normal forces required to maintain the stack's equilibrium can become unstable with minimal movement. You should always include a margin about the load and maintain smooth ramps and transition areas to prevent these destabilizing vibrations.",
      "source": "target",
