      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Microscopic shifts often result from subtle imperfections in the beam-to-pallet interface that don't appear visually but generate enough cumulative stress to break frictional adhesion. Additionally, thermal expansion and contraction in the Rotterdam climate can cause minute wood or steel deformation, creating internal pressures that suddenly force a load out of place. It is also possible that the \"micro-nudging\" comes from the forklift carriage itself, as pallets tightly packed against beams can act as rollers, allowing them to glide rather than lock securely when the rack carriages are adjusted. Checking for proper bearing friction and ensuring beams are sufficiently grounded to prevent wheel slip is critical to stopping these random movements.",
